(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["pharmacology-i-exam-2-pharm-e2-drill-glaucoma-diagnostics"]={"title":"Glaucoma & Diagnostics Drill","category":"Pharmacology I","exam":"Exam 2","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which class is FIRST LINE for glaucoma and the most commonly used?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Dorzolamide","Pilocarpine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The prostaglandin analogues, which increase aqueous outflow. Beta blockers are second line. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class changes EYELASH LENGTH and IRIS COLOUR?","choices":["Brimonidine","Bimatoprost","Betaxolol","Brinzolamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The prostaglandin analogues, which is why one of them is also marketed for lash growth. They also cause conjunctival hyperaemia. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Why must a prostaglandin analogue NOT be dosed more than once daily?","choices":["It causes rebound hyperaemia","It raises the risk of cataract","Exceeding once daily INHIBITS the pressure-lowering effect","It causes bradycardia"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. More is worse, not better, which is counterintuitive and therefore worth holding. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 66"},{"q":"Which glaucoma agent is BETA 1 SELECTIVE?","choices":["Timolol","Carteolol","Levobunolol","Betaxolol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The selective one, so it carries less risk of bronchoconstriction in a patient with asthma. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Why is a NONSELECTIVE beta blocker more efficacious in the eye but worse tolerated?","choices":["There are more beta 2 receptors in the eye","There are more beta 1 receptors in the eye","It penetrates the cornea better","It has a longer half-life"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. So blocking both gives more effect, at the cost of beta 2 effects elsewhere. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class risks worsening heart failure, bradycardia, heart block and increased airway resistance?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Dorzolamide","Pilocarpine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Systemic beta blockade from a drop, which is why the nasolacrimal duct is worth occluding. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class works by blocking beta receptors in the ciliary body epithelium to reduce aqueous production?","choices":["Travoprost","Carbachol","Levobunolol","Apraclonidine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Less catecholamine activation, less cyclic AMP, less aqueous made. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 67"},{"q":"Which glaucoma agent is CONTRAINDICATED in children under two years of age?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Dorzolamide","Brimonidine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The alpha-2 agonists, because of central nervous system depression and apnoea. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which alpha-2 agonist is MORE LIPOPHILIC?","choices":["Brimonidine","Apraclonidine","Timolol","Pilocarpine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Apraclonidine is highly ionised at physiological pH; brimonidine is the more lipophilic of the two, and allergic conjunctivitis is less common with it. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Which class reduces aqueous production by inhibiting carbonic anhydrase in the ciliary body epithelium?","choices":["Bimatoprost","Dorzolamide","Betaxolol","Carbachol"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Less bicarbonate, less fluid transport, lower pressure. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class causes a BITTER TASTE in about a quarter of patients and stinging in about a third?","choices":["Latanoprost","Timolol","Brinzolamide","Brimonidine"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The carbonic anhydrase inhibitors, which also cause allergic conjunctivitis. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 70"},{"q":"Which class activates muscarinic receptors, contracting the ciliary muscle to facilitate outflow?","choices":["Timolol","Brimonidine","Dorzolamide","Pilocarpine"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The cholinergic agonists. Compliance is poor because of side effects and frequent administration. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which glaucoma class causes fixed, small pupils, myopia and visual disturbance — and is especially poorly tolerated by YOUNGER patients?","choices":["Carbachol","Latanoprost","Brinzolamide","Betaxolol"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The cholinergic agonists. Younger patients are usually intolerant of miotic therapy because of the visual blurring. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which cholinergic agonist is used in the SURGICAL setting?","choices":["Pilocarpine","Acetylcholine","Carbachol","Tropicamide"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The surgical agent among the three cholinergic agonists. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"A patient's pressure is not controlled on one agent. What is the principle behind adding a second?","choices":["Choose an agent of the same class at a higher strength","Add a topical glucocorticoid","Choose a DIFFERENT mechanism, for synergy and fewer drops","Add a topical vasoconstrictor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Targeting multiple routes lowers pressure synergistically, and fewer drops improves compliance. Adding a drug of the same category would be the wrong move. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"What is the general goal of glaucoma therapy, in terms of pressure?","choices":["A 50 per cent reduction","Any reduction at all","Normalising to below 10 mmHg","A 20 to 30 per cent reduction"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. And therapy can be started in one eye to judge efficacy and tolerability. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which patients with ocular hypertension are treated rather than monitored?","choices":["Those WITH risk factors","All patients with raised pressure","Only those already blind in one eye","Only those under 40"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Those without risk factors are monitored until glaucomatous changes occur. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which ocular anaesthetic leaves the eye numb for 10 to 20 minutes with NO BLINK REFLEX?","choices":["Tropicamide","Proparacaine","Phenylephrine","Fluorescein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. True of both ocular anaesthetics, and the reason you must not write prescriptions for them. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which ocular agents should you NOT write prescriptions for?","choices":["Latanoprost","Olopatadine","Tetracaine","Erythromycin"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The ocular anaesthetics. Used in clinic for tonometry, foreign body removal and superficial corneal surgery, but not sent home. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which class competitively blocks muscarinic receptors to produce mydriasis for a fundoscopic examination?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Pilocarpine","Proparacaine","Tropicamide"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The antimuscarinic cycloplegics, with atropine and cyclopentolate. Also used in uveitis to prevent synechiae and relieve ciliary spasm. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 76"},{"q":"A pupil dilated with which agent stays MORE REACTIVE TO LIGHT?","choices":["Phenylephrine","Atropine","Cyclopentolate","Tropicamide"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The sympathomimetic mydriatic leaves more light reactivity than the antimuscarinics do. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 77"},{"q":"Which agent reveals epithelial defects of the cornea and conjunctiva?","choices":["Proparacaine","Fluorescein","Tropicamide","Phenylephrine"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Used for anterior segment staining and disclosing corneal injury. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 78"},{"q":"What is the normal range of intraocular pressure?","choices":["About 30 to 40 mmHg","About 2 to 8 mmHg","About 10 to 21 mmHg","About 40 to 60 mmHg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Ocular hypertension is a pressure above normal WITHOUT optic nerve damage or visual field loss. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Glaucoma drugs focus on which type, and why?","choices":["Closed angle, because it is more dangerous","Both equally","Neither — glaucoma is surgical","Open angle — that is where drug therapy works"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Angle-closure is an abrupt, emergent blockage of the drainage canal, usually with acute pain, and is not primarily a drug problem. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 60"},{"q":"Which two routes lower intraocular pressure, and which class does BOTH?","choices":["Brimonidine — it increases outflow AND decreases production","Latanoprost — it does both","Timolol — it does both","Dorzolamide — it does both"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alpha adrenergic agonists appear on both lists. Prostaglandins and cholinergics increase outflow; beta blockers and carbonic anhydrase inhibitors decrease production. — Ophthalmology-2.pptx, Slide 64"}]};
